      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Uptown Saturday Night (1974) marked a pivotal moment in Black cinema, with Sidney Poitier directing and starring alongside Bill Cosby in a comedy-crime caper that defied stereotypical roles. The film follows two working-class friends who get robbed at an illegal nightclub and must retrieve a winning lottery ticket. Unlike the serious dramatic roles Poitier was known for in films like <a href=\"/movie/10633\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"10633\">In the Heat of the Night</a> (1967), this showcased his comedic talents while paving the way for future Black buddy comedies like <a href=\"/movie/150\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"150\">48 Hrs.</a> (1982) and <a href=\"/movie/941\" class=\"movie-title\" data-tmdb-id=\"941\">Lethal Weapon</a> (1987)."
      },
      {
